  .golangci/ruleguard/modern.go: gocritic's ruleguard rules, which are DATA rather than a package any build compiles. No pass reaches it because every pass lints `./...`, and the Go toolchain skips a dot-directory: `go list ./.golangci/...` matches no packages. The dot is deliberate and is what keeps the rules out of every Ze build. It is not unchecked: .golangci.yml points gocritic's ruleguard checker at this exact path with `failOn: all`, so the file is parsed on EVERY pass and a malformed rule stops the run rather than silently dropping every rule in it. What no pass does is run the analyzers OVER it. It stops being blind when a pass can name a package explicitly -- `go list -tags ruleguard ./.golangci/ruleguard` resolves, and the dsl is vendored -- which needs a package selector on Flavor (matrix.go), a field the type does not have because every other flavor is a GOOS, GOARCH and tag set over one fixed pattern
  examples/plugin/go/main.go: a separate Go module (`module example/acme-monitor`), so `./...` cannot reach it and golangci-lint would need its own run in that directory. It has no tracked go.sum, so the module does not resolve without `go mod tidy`, and this repository vendors its dependencies rather than fetching them. Needs an owner decision on how the example module joins the build
  tools.go: the tools.go idiom: a `//go:build tools` file whose imports are PROGRAMS, kept so `go mod tidy` pins the build tools. golangci-lint reports 'is a program, not an importable package' for each one, and so would any type checker. It stops being blind when the pins move to go.mod's `tool` directives, which is its own change
